# Listora local configuration. Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and adjust; every key is optional
# for local development unless marked required. Never put real credentials in this file.

# --- Local Postgres (scripts/dev-db.sh) ---
# TCP port of this checkout's cluster in .local/pg. Change it if another worktree or server
# already uses 55432; DATABASE_URL and TEST_DATABASE_URL below must use the same port.
LISTORA_PG_PORT=55432

# --- API (apps/api) ---
# Required. Postgres connection URL for the API, the worker, `npm run db:migrate`, and
# `npm run admin`.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://listora@127.0.0.1:55432/listora
# Interface and port the API listens on (0 picks a free port).
API_HOST=127.0.0.1
API_PORT=3000
# Required. How users sign in: `dev` prints a one-time sign-in code to the API log (local
# development only); `cognito` verifies tokens of the Cognito user pool (staging and production;
# see docs/auth.md).
AUTH_DRIVER=dev
# Key that signs dev session tokens, at least 32 characters (for example the output of
# `openssl rand -base64 32`). Leave it unset to use a random key per process; sessions then end
# whenever the API restarts. Only the dev driver reads it.
# AUTH_DEV_SECRET=
# Required when AUTH_DRIVER=cognito, ignored otherwise. The user pool id (its region comes from
# the id) and a comma-separated list of the app client ids whose tokens the API accepts. Neither
# is a secret. The worker reads COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID too (see below).
# COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID=us-east-1_AbC123xyZ
# COGNITO_CLIENT_IDS=

# --- Photo storage (packages/storage; read by the API and the worker, which must share it) ---
# Where photos are stored: local (files under STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR, served by the API; the default)
# or s3 (production: presigned uploads to S3, public images through CloudFront).
STORAGE_DRIVER=local
# Local driver: directory for stored files. Defaults to .local/storage at the repository root.
# STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR=
# Local driver: the API's URL as clients reach it; upload and image URLs start with it. Keep it
# in step with API_HOST and API_PORT (on a device, use this Mac's LAN address instead).
STORAGE_LOCAL_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000
# Local driver: key that signs upload URLs, at least 32 characters (for example the output of
# `openssl rand -base64 32`). Unset means a random key per process, so upload URLs stop working
# when the API restarts; `getUploadSession` then hands out new ones.
# STORAGE_LOCAL_SIGNING_SECRET=
# S3 driver (required): bucket, its region, and the CloudFront URL that serves its public/ keys.
# Credentials come from the default AWS provider chain (the ECS task role in production), never
# from this file.
# STORAGE_S3_BUCKET=
# STORAGE_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
# S3 driver: another S3-compatible endpoint (path-style addressing). Defaults to AWS.
# STORAGE_S3_ENDPOINT=

# --- Worker (apps/worker) ---
# The worker also reads DATABASE_URL, the photo storage keys, and the AI keys. Marketplace
# operation steps it runs at the same time (1 to 100).
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=5
# Photo processing and item analysis jobs it runs at the same time (1 to 100), in a pool of
# their own, so slow AI calls never hold the slots an urgent delist needs.
WORKER_TASK_CONCURRENCY=2
# Development and tests only; leave unset in production. Base URL of the fake marketplace
# (`npm run dev:fake-marketplace` listens on this URL's port), which Depop listings whose
# connection uses the API go through while DEPOP_ENV (below) is unset, and eBay listings while
# EBAY_ENV is unset. With neither, eBay operations fail with a Retry action instead of waiting.
# Poshmark, Mercari, and Depop are served assisted (user tasks) with or without it, until the
# browser runner (decision D1) lands.
# FAKE_MARKETPLACE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4100
# COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID (above): the account deletion job deletes the pool's users
# (cognito-idp:AdminGetUser and AdminDeleteUser, with AWS credentials from the default AWS credential chain, the
# ECS task role, never from this file). Without it, deletions of Cognito users wait and the
# worker logs an error; dev users need none.

# --- eBay (packages/marketplaces; read by the worker and the eBay commands) ---
# Which eBay the worker lists on: fake (the fake eBay server, `npm run dev:fake-ebay`), sandbox,
# or production. Unset: no eBay adapter (eBay listings then use FAKE_MARKETPLACE_URL when it is
# set, and otherwise fail with a Retry action). Nothing below is needed with fake.
EBAY_ENV=fake
# fake only: the fake eBay's URL (`npm run dev:fake-ebay` listens on its port).
EBAY_FAKE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4200
# sandbox and production (required there): the keyset's App ID (client id) and Cert ID (client
# secret) from developer.ebay.com > My Account > Application Keys. Secrets: never commit them.
# EBAY_CLIENT_ID=
# EBAY_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The keyset's RuName (eBay redirect URL name) under User Tokens; `npm run ebay:sandbox-token`
# uses it for the seller's consent, and eBay connect (T-16) will too.
# EBAY_RUNAME=
# fake and sandbox only (refused in production, where each eBay connection gets its own token
# with eBay connect, T-16): one seller for development and the sandbox check. The seller's
# refresh token (about 18 months; `npm run ebay:sandbox-token` writes it here; a secret; with
# fake, the fake's development seller when unset), and the id of the one Listora eBay connection
# (a `marketplace_connection` row) that seller lists for. Without EBAY_USER_CONNECTION_ID no
# seller is configured. Listings on any other connection, or on none, fail with "Connect eBay",
# as every eBay operation does in production until eBay connect.
# EBAY_USER_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# EBAY_USER_CONNECTION_ID=
# The seller's business policy ids (Seller Hub > Business policies), all three or none. None:
# listings get free USPS Priority shipping and no returns.
# EBAY_FULFILLMENT_POLICY_ID=
# EBAY_PAYMENT_POLICY_ID=
# EBAY_RETURN_POLICY_ID=
# The US ZIP code items ship from, and business days to ship (0 to 3). Defaults 95125 and 3.
# EBAY_ITEM_POSTAL_CODE=95125
# EBAY_DISPATCH_DAYS=3
# The application's daily Trading API call limit until eBay reports it (eBay's default is
# 5,000; the Application Growth Check raises it). Each worker also reads the real usage.
# EBAY_TRADING_DAILY_CALL_LIMIT=5000
# Sandbox check only: a public https photo eBay can fetch for the test listing.
# EBAY_SANDBOX_PHOTO_URL=

# --- Depop (packages/marketplaces; read by the worker) ---
# Depop runs assisted (user tasks) until Listora's Depop partner access is granted and a seller
# connects Depop with OAuth; only listings on a connection whose mode is `api` use the Selling
# API. Which Depop those listings go to: fake (the fake Depop, `npm run dev:fake-depop`),
# sandbox (Depop's staging API), or production. Unset: no Depop API adapter (api-connected
# listings then use FAKE_MARKETPLACE_URL when it is set, and otherwise stay assisted). Nothing
# below is needed with fake.
# DEPOP_ENV=fake
# fake only: the fake Depop's URL (`npm run dev:fake-depop` listens on its port).
# DEPOP_FAKE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4300
# sandbox and production (required there): Listora's OAuth client from Depop
# (business@depop.com). Secret: never commit it.
# DEPOP_CLIENT_ID=
# DEPOP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# fake and sandbox only (refused in production, where each Depop connection gets its own token
# when the seller connects Depop): one seller for development. The seller's refresh token (a
# secret; with fake, the fake's development seller when unset), and the id of the one Listora
# Depop connection (a `marketplace_connection` row with mode `api`) it lists for. Depop rotates
# refresh tokens on every refresh and this one is kept only in the worker's memory, so after a
# worker restart a sandbox seller consents again (the fake renews its development seller).
# Listings on any other connection fail with "Connect Depop".
# DEPOP_USER_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# DEPOP_USER_CONNECTION_ID=
# Where the configured seller's items are: a two-letter country (default US) and a state.
# DEPOP_ITEM_COUNTRY=US
# DEPOP_ITEM_STATE=
# How they ship, required with DEPOP_USER_CONNECTION_ID outside fake. Depop Shipping (most US
# sellers can't ship manually): the shop address id (GET /api/v1/shop/seller-addresses/), and
# a provider and parcel size its shipping providers offer, and who pays (buyer or seller)...
# DEPOP_SHIP_FROM_ADDRESS_ID=
# DEPOP_SHIPPING_PROVIDER_ID=USPS
# DEPOP_PARCEL_SIZE_ID=
# DEPOP_SHIPPING_PAYER=buyer
# ...or, instead, a national shipping cost such as 4.99.
# DEPOP_NATIONAL_SHIPPING_COST=
# This process's share of Depop's application-wide limit of 30,000 requests a minute: split it
# between the workers when more than one runs.
# DEPOP_APP_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=30000

# --- All backend processes ---
# One of fatal, error, warn, info, debug, trace, silent.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- AI (packages/ai) ---
# Which AI provider serves calls: fake (fixture answers, no network, the default) or anthropic.
AI_PROVIDER=fake
# Required when AI_PROVIDER=anthropic. Never commit a real key; tests that need it skip without it.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Model per tier; each must be in the current price table (packages/ai/src/prices.ts).
AI_MODEL_STANDARD=claude-haiku-5-5
AI_MODEL_PREMIUM=claude-sonnet-5-5
# Per-account daily AI spend cap in US dollars (UTC days); calls stop once it is reached.
AI_DAILY_BUDGET_USD=1.00
# Deploy-time global kill switch: on refuses every AI call for every account. Operators also
# have a switch in the database, changed with `npm run admin -- ai-kill-switch on|off`; either
# one being on refuses calls.
AI_KILL_SWITCH=off
# Per-request timeout for the Anthropic API, in milliseconds (1000 to 600000). The default,
# 600000, is the SDK's own and leaves room for long Premium calls.
AI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
# AI spend check (the worker runs it hourly; `npm run admin -- ai-alerts check` runs it once):
# alert when AI spend by all accounts together in one UTC day reaches this many US dollars
# (above zero). Its per-account limit is AI_DAILY_BUDGET_USD above.
AI_GLOBAL_DAILY_ALERT_USD=25.00
# The check warns that an account or the platform is approaching a limit (the daily budget, a
# plan quota, or the global threshold) from this percentage of it (an integer from 1 to 100).
AI_ALERT_WARN_PERCENT=80
# Directory of fake-provider fixtures (<photo-set hash>.json, plus an optional default.json for
# photo sets without their own file). Defaults to packages/ai/fixtures.
# AI_FAKE_FIXTURES_DIR=

# --- Item analysis (packages/analysis) ---
# on: when a fast analysis asks for escalation (uncertain brand or product, designer or vintage
# look, or the model asks), the analysis job queues the premium identification itself, if the
# plan's premium quota allows. An unidentified brand only suggests it. off: the user starts
# it ("Identify harder"). Read by the analysis job in the worker.
AI_AUTO_ESCALATE=on

# --- Infrastructure deploys (infra/; read by `cdk synth` / `cdk deploy`, never by the app) ---
# Not needed for local development or `npm run check`, which synthesize with placeholders.
# Each can also be passed as CDK context (`-c env=staging`), which wins over the variable.
# The deploy workflow sets them from the GitHub environment's variables. See infra/README.md.
# Environment to synthesize or deploy: staging, prod, or personal (the low-cost single-user
# setup priced in docs/costs.md).
# LISTORA_ENV=staging
# 12-digit id of that environment's AWS account (each environment has its own account).
# LISTORA_AWS_ACCOUNT_ID=
# AWS region; SES must receive email there. Defaults to us-east-1.
# LISTORA_AWS_REGION=us-east-1
# The environment's DNS name (API at api.<domain>, sale emails at in.<domain>). Required for
# staging and prod; optional for personal, which without one serves the API at its default
# execute-api URL and receives no sale email (infra/README.md, Without a domain).
# LISTORA_DOMAIN=staging.example.com
# Route 53 public hosted zone id for LISTORA_DOMAIN; set with it, or not at all.
# LISTORA_HOSTED_ZONE_ID=
# Application image tag to run (the git commit); `latest` is refused.
# LISTORA_IMAGE_TAG=
# Optional address subscribed to the CloudWatch alarm topic.
# LISTORA_ALARM_EMAIL=
# GitHub owner/name allowed to deploy through OIDC; defaults to toniojbusiness/listora.
# LISTORA_GITHUB_REPOSITORY=
# Optional: the two availability zones the VPC spans, comma-separated. Unset means the region's
# first two. Set it when one of those is a zone some service skips (in us-east-1, use1-az3 has
# no API Gateway VPC links, which the personal environment uses). See infra/README.md.
# LISTORA_AVAILABILITY_ZONES=us-east-1a,us-east-1b

# --- iOS app (apps/ios) ---
# The app does not read this file. Its API_BASE_URL is set per build configuration in
# apps/ios/Config/*.xcconfig; Dev uses http://127.0.0.1:3000 to match API_HOST and API_PORT.
# Override it per checkout in apps/ios/Config/Local.xcconfig (gitignored).
#
# `make ios-testflight` (a local TestFlight upload; "Releases" in apps/ios/README.md) reads
# these from the shell environment, not from this file. In GitHub Actions they are secrets of
# the ios-staging and ios-prod environments. Never write their values into any file in the repo.
# Key ID of the App Store Connect team API key (Admin role).
# APP_STORE_CONNECT_KEY_ID=
# Issuer ID shown above the team keys in App Store Connect (Users and Access > Integrations).
# APP_STORE_CONNECT_ISSUER_ID=
# The whole contents of the key's AuthKey_<key id>.p8 file, for example "$(cat AuthKey_X.p8)".
# APP_STORE_CONNECT_PRIVATE_KEY=
# 10-character Apple Developer team id (developer.apple.com > Account > Membership details).
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=

# --- Tests ---
# Database for integration tests. Defaults to the listora_test database on LISTORA_PG_PORT.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://listora@127.0.0.1:55432/listora_test
